 "result": "Call order when a view raises (all in `src/tessel/`):\n\n1. **`app.py` – the dispatch method around line 1012** (the one containing `run_view`) catches the exception and calls `route_user_fault(ctx, e)`.\n2. **`app.py:865` `route_user_fault`**:\n   - An `HTTPException` that `trap_http_exception` doesn't trap goes to `route_http_fault` (`app.py:830`).\n   - Otherwise it calls `_pick_fault_handler`.\n   - With no handler found, it re-raises.\n3. **`app.py:830` `route_http_fault`** calls `_pick_fault_handler`, and falls back to returning the exception itself as the response if there is no handler.\n4. **`sansio/app.py:869` `_pick_fault_handler`** does the selection:\n   - It resolves the exception's class and HTTP code via `_get_exc_class_and_code`.\n   - It looks in `error_handler_spec[name][code_or_None]` for each bundle name in `ctx.request.bundles`, then for the app (`None`).\n   - It searches code-specific handlers first, then handlers with no code.\n   - Within each map it walks the exception class's MRO, so the most specific class wins.\n   - Bundle handlers take priority over app handlers at each level.\n5. **`app.py:897` `route_unhandled_fault`** runs if re-raised or if error handling itself fails. It is called from `wsgi_entry` (`app.py:1600`). It logs, wraps the error as a 500, and uses `_pick_fault_handler` again (`app.py:943`) for a 500/`InternalServerError` handler.\n\nHandlers get into `error_handler_spec` via `register_error_handler` (`sansio/scaffold.py:650`).",
